course: dictionary
lesson: 218
title: Alibi: proof and excuses
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An alibi is proof that a person was somewhere else when a crime happened, so they could not have done it. «Giving a false alibi about where someone was can put them in danger if something goes wrong.» Here a false alibi is a story that is not true, told to cover where someone really was.
explain: Detectives in stories check each suspect's alibi. They ask, "Where were you at that time, and can anyone prove it?" A strong alibi is good proof, and a cast-iron alibi is very strong proof.
explain: In everyday talk, alibi can just mean an excuse. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits: is someone proving where they were, or simply giving a reason for something?
fact: An alibi is proof that a person was somewhere else when a crime happened.
fact: «Alibi» can also mean an excuse, in informal talk.
fact: Phrases to know: a strong alibi, a cast-iron alibi, check an alibi.
fact: «False» means not true, so a false alibi is a lie about where someone was.
fact: Context clues help: a detective story points you to the law meaning, and words like "excuse" point to the everyday meaning.
exercise: In a detective story, what is an alibi? | A clue left at the scene of a crime | A kind of lock on a door | *Proof that a person was somewhere else when a crime happened
exercise: «Giving a false alibi about where someone was can put them in danger.» What does false mean here? | Written down carefully | Done in a hurry | *Not true, a lie | Very kind and helpful
exercise: Tom was late for school. His alibi was that the bus broke down. What does alibi mean here? | *An excuse | A friend who lives nearby | A school timetable | A map of the bus route
test: A cast-iron alibi is very strong proof. What does a strong alibi show? | *That the person was somewhere else when the crime happened | That the person is very rich | That the person is a detective | That the person was very tired
test: The police check each suspect's alibi. What does check an alibi mean? | Lock it away in a safe | Make up a brand new story | Forget about it completely | *Look into whether their proof of being elsewhere is true
exam: Dan says, «My alibi is that I was at football, so I could not have broken the window.» What does alibi mean in his words? | A football match on television | *His proof or excuse for being somewhere else | A very loud shout | The window he broke
